## Load Balancer and AWS EC2

A common AWS architecture is to place multiple EC2 instances behind a load balancer.

```text
                    Internet
                       |
                       ▼
                 Load Balancer
                       |
             ┌─────────┼─────────┐
             ▼         ▼         ▼
           EC2-1     EC2-2     EC2-3
             |         |         |
             └─────────┼─────────┘
                       ▼
                  Application
```

This is useful because if one EC2 instance fails, other instances can continue serving requests.

---

## Load Balancer and Auto Scaling

Load balancing becomes even more useful when combined with **Auto Scaling**.

Suppose an application normally has two servers:

```text
Load Balancer
      |
   ┌──┴──┐
   ▼     ▼
 EC2    EC2
```

During a traffic spike, Auto Scaling can launch additional instances:

```text
Load Balancer
      |
 ┌────┼────┬────┐
 ▼    ▼    ▼    ▼
EC2  EC2  EC2  EC2
```

The new instances can then become available to receive traffic.

When traffic decreases, unnecessary instances can be removed.

So the combination provides:

**Load Balancer → Traffic Distribution**

**Auto Scaling → Adjust Number of Servers**

Together, they help create a scalable and highly available application.

